  "id": "hip-hinge",
  "name": "Hip Hinge",
  "definition": "A bilateral unsupported closed-chain barbell good morning in which the athlete begins upright with a free bar across the posterior shoulder and upper-back surface, hinges to the deepest reviewed position that preserves the instructed natural spine technique, and returns to standing through combined hip and spinal extension. The initial roster is limited to Schellenberg et al.'s eight-repetition fixture with shoulder-width, slightly toe-out feet and external barbell load equal to 25 percent of body mass. Direct three-dimensional measurements showed a small but nonzero mean knee excursion, so the knee is neither called position-held nor promoted to a prime action. The same measurements showed material pelvis-lumbar and lumbar-thoracic excursion, so spinal extension is an explicit prime rather than being hidden behind a posture cue. Medial hamstrings, gluteus maximus, and lumbar extensors are non-ranked co-primary contributors. The visible biceps-femoris region receives only knee-control credit because its unsplit mesh cannot distinguish the hip-extending long head from the monoarticular short head. Romanian, stiff-leg, conventional, sumo, trap-bar, deficit, unilateral, staggered, dumbbell, cable, Smith, floor-pull, swing, and externally supported back-extension variants remain outside this contract.",
  "evidenceRefs": [
    "arnold-2010-lower-limb",
    "christophy-2012-lumbar-spine",
    "schellenberg-2013-deadlift-goodmorning-kinematics"
  ],
